  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1158 AM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nA closed upper level low positioned over Saskatchewan/ND/MT border \nby 00Z will continue to track slowly off towards the east/southeast \nskirting across south central Canada and ND/MN (becoming an open \nwave) through Sunday evening along with its very positive surface \ntrough. REFS/HRRR keeps the scattered snow showers north of the CWA \nwith this system with overall dry weather expected through the rest \nof the weekend. However, HRRR indicates the possibility of spotty \nlight snow or flurries on the backside of this system Sunday \nevening, mainly over the far portions of north NESD/MN per lower \nclouds clipping over this area. Latest run of the NBM added these \nsnow chances and went ahead and blended in some HRRR as well with \npops of 15-25%. No accumulation is expected. Otherwise high pressure \nwill filter in from the northwest behind this system Sunday evening \nwith the 1035mb high over the region by 12Z Monday. It will track \nsoutheast (and strengthen) Monday and Monday night, forecast to be \nover ~IA by 12Z Tuesday.\n\nOur next weak shortwave is forecast to track west to east across the \nregion Tuesday evening into Wednesday in combination with a west to \neast oriented weak surface trough moving in on the backside of this \nhigh across the state. While still a few days out, mean \nGEFS/ENS/GEPS does indicate the potential for light snow late \nTuesday night into Wednesday morning mainly east of the Mo River \nwith ENS indicating the \"highest potential\" James Valley and \neastward. 00z run of the EC meteograms only indicates about a 20% of \nsnow at KABR and up to 35% at KATY, with less than 5% chance across \ncentral SD. Latest NBM run matches well thus far with a 15-20% \nchance of snow or rain/snow mix James Valley and eastward. \nProbability of snow>0.1\" per mean ensemble of GEFS/ENS/GEPS is 20-\n35% for this area, with a 15-20% of snow over a half inch (highest \nover Coteau). NBM has this chance about 20% for 0.1\"+ and 10% or \nless for 0.5\"+. Any snow that does fall is expected to remain light \nwith impacts minimal to none for the holiday travel.\n\nCooler air will filter into the region tonight through early next \nweek with winds out of the northwest at 850mb. The coldest temps at \nthis level of -5 to -10C will be Sunday evening through Monday \nmorning and coldest over northern and NESD per incoming high. These \ntemps run within the 15-20th percentile for this time of year. With \nclouds forecast to clear out west to east across the CWA after \nmidnight, overnight lows will range in the lower to upper 20s, \ncoldest over north central SD. 25th percentile temps is upper teens \nto the mid 20s if we get clearing faster and more time for \nradiational cooling. However, winds will keep the boundary layer \nmixed so not expected to drop too much lower than the mean. Highs \nfor Sunday will range in the 30s with lows by Monday morning down in \nthe the mid to upper teens when this coldest air is overhead. With \nthis cold high over the region Monday, highs will range in the upper \n20s to the mid 30s (spread of 3-4 degrees per NBM 25-75th for max \nT). This runs near to slightly below average for this time of year. \nAs this high moves out we will see \"warmer air\" filter in from the \nsouthwest to the northeast across the CWA with temps gradually \nwarming into the upper 30s to lower 40s by the midweek.",
